p {}, so the plan gate \"\n566:                \"cannot tell whose claim copies to read; squash the lane's own \"\n573:def _claim_base(root, branch):\n574:    \"\"\"The lane's claim base: its integration base, or HEAD when that cannot\n575:    be read and the lane holds no claim folder (trunk). None holds. With the\n576:    trunk undeclared, a branch whose HEAD holds no claim folder is judged\n614:            base = _claim_base(root, branch)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"git show e62eea418778b8ccf35ad484b8a484bf259d77c3:project-trajectory/skills/coordinator-cycle/SKILL.md | Select-String '\"'^## 4'\"' -Context 0,95; rg -n 'check_doc_refs|strict' docs/stack.ini | Select-Object -First 35; git show e62eea418778b8ccf35ad484b8a484bf259d77c3:project-trajectory/scripts/check_doc_refs.py | Select-Object -Skip 585 -First 85\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"git show e62eea418778b8ccf35ad484b8a484bf259d77c3:project-trajectory/skills/coordinator-cycle/SKILL.md | Select-String '\"'^## 4'\"' -Context 0,95; rg -n 'check_doc_refs|strict' docs/stack.ini | Select-Object -First 35; git show e62eea418778b8ccf35ad484b8a484bf259d77c3:project-trajectory/scripts/check_doc_refs.py | Select-Object -Skip 585 -First 85\"","aggregated_output":"\r\n> ## 4. Land\r\n  \r\n  Parallel lanes are normal; adjudication is a latched event. A lane's verdicts\r\n  may be sat at any time; its acts keep landing order (revisions 31 and 32).\r\n  Take a lane's act turn only after the lanes ahead of it have landed and it has\r\n  rebased, so its acts and the id watermark pull up as each lane lands. A lane\r\n  that took acts on an older base gets duplicate act seqs on rebase, and git\r\n  applies the later act commits cleanly. Lanes that take acts land one at a time.\r\n  Before the act turn, check that the rows the verdict judged are unchanged since\r\n  it judged them: the rows at the verdict's own commit, before the rebase,\r\n  against the rebased tip. A renumbered row counts as changed; re-sit any changed\r\n  row (recipes §3).\r\n  \r\n  Two landing rules for the machinery itself (owner, 2026-10-14; revision 37):\r\n  \r\n  - **A lane that changes the machinery the cycle runs on lands alone.** The\r\n    next such lane lands only after the cycle has run through the last one's\r\n    changed step.\r\n  - **A defect in a landed mechanism change is fixed forward,** in a lane with\r\n    a regression test, never by reverting a landing. A step the defect blocks\r\n    runs by hand, attended and recorded, never through a bypass flag or a\r\n    fallback path. A hook that refuses the fix commit is the owner's to decide.\r\n  \r\n  1. Rebase onto current trunk before the final act. Use recipes §3–§4; if the\r\n     rebase changes code or authored rows, repeat the affected checkpoint and\r\n     run an uncounted convergence round over the rebased tip.\r\n  2. After the rebase and the unchanged-rows check, take the act turn,\r\n     `coordinator_adjudicate.py act --verdict <path> --wi WI-NNN` (recipes §3).\r\n     Its act is the lane's last **spine** commit: Status flips and the snapshot\r\n     only, with verdicts committed first. Telemetry may follow.\r\n  3. Squash, regenerate, run the commit bar on the staged squash, close the\r\n     row, archive the tips, sweep, and close the re-mint citing the act. Every\r\n     step and its trap is in recipes §3.\r\n  4. File one successor row on trunk per SUCCESSOR ruling of an accepted\r\n     dispute verdict, from its binding (recipes §3).\r\n  \r\n  ## 5. Decisions and the owner\r\n  \r\n  Record calls not already settled by the spec or a ruled item in\r\n  `docs/decisions/<branch>.toml` (`coordinator-<date>.toml` for calls on trunk),\r\n  with the riskiest in `high_risk`. Ask the owner to **confirm or overrule**,\r\n  never to approve. A decision only the owner can make, and that blocks a row,\r\n  becomes a pending open item cited by the row's `needs` in the same commit; then\r\n  move to the next row. Stop that row for an owner signature on a new need, a\r\n  frame external crossing or an explicit owner checkpoint. A high-risk finding\r\n  goes to the owner. Leave held signatures and open-item rulings to the owner,\r\n  along with push and merge to main.\r\n  \r\n  ## 6. Close-out\r\n  \r\n  Follow `session-protocol` §4, \"Coordinator close-out\", including the lease\r\n  handback or relaunch, and its blackout close-down inside an armed window.\r\n  Record procedural corrections here or in the recipes.\r\n  The handoff links the one it replaces, points here for procedure, and holds\r\n  state only: what landed, what is open, what the owner owes, and the session\r\n  prompt. It carries no cycle, recipe or \"corrections learned\" section.\r\n  \r\n  **Record the session's usage** before the handoff is written, from the\r\n  primary checkout (`$PY` and `$SCRIPTS` as in the recipes' section 0):\r\n  \r\n  ```bash\r\n  \"$PY\" \"$SCRIPTS/usage_ingest.py\"\r\n  \"$PY\" \"$SCRIPTS/usage_ingest.py\" --session <the replaced handoff's session id>\r\n  ```\r\n  \r\n  - The first run records this session and every sub-agent it spawned into\r\n    the spool. The second records what the previous session wrote after its\r\n    own last run (its closing reply, or a sub-agent message that was still\r\n    open then); skip it when the replaced handoff names no session id. A\r\n    repeated run records nothing twice.\r\n  - The handoff names this session's id (`$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`) under its\r\n    state, so the next close-out can pick up this session's tail. A nonzero\r\n    exit is recorded there too, with the lines it printed; it is never\r\n    hand-patched.\r\n  \r\n  ## 7. Headless legs\r\n  \r\n  This section is the headless procedure's one home. It carries the review-churn\r\n  plan's revisions 35 (\"The coordinator role\") and 36 (\"Headless coordinator\r\n  legs are marked now\"), the owner's 2026-10-14 split ruling (WI-918 takes the\r\n  merge slot's reading of the re-claim), and\r\n  `docs/decisions/coordinator-2026-10-14.toml` D-015 (the lane-mode reading) and\r\n  D-016 (the design calls).\r\n  \r\n  **A headless leg is an autonomous run.** The overnight launcher\r\n  (`scripts/coordinator/overnight_legs.py`) marks the run once, before its first\r\n  leg: one loop session per run, inherited by every leg and every process a leg\r\n  starts. Every leg's prompt is the handoff's session prompt, then the\r\n  [headless block](../../../scripts/coordinator/headless-leg.md), then the kit's\r\n  provenance line naming the session. After each leg the launcher audits every\r\n  ref the leg moved and stops the run, naming the commit or the read, when one\r\n  fails its rule. It judges every leg against the trunk it bound at run start,\r\n  and stops the run when a leg leaves the primary checkout on any other branch:\r\n  a leg's work stays in lane worktrees.\r\n  \r\n  **The trailer on every commit:**\r\n162:# byte-preserving move out of agent_loop's string constants, the strict\r\n287:# `--strict` exit contract, the absent-registry vacuous case and the live-repo\r\n457:# subagent-gate parse-failure arm's more-restrictive rule\r\n1058:# --strict gates on dangling alone, and the untraced COUNT still prints on every\r\n1065:command = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_doc_refs.py --root . --strict --record-prefix docs/log.md --record-prefix docs/archive/ --record-prefix docs/reviews/ --record-prefix docs/plans/ --record-prefix docs/repo-review- --record-prefix docs/test/report.md --record-prefix docs/ai-template-redesign-\r\n1077:# --strict gates at DevStg-Impl beside the other product-layer quality checks.\r\n1081:command = {py} project-trajectory/scripts/check_figures.py --root . --strict\r\n1100:# a gate even under --strict, and this baseline is DOWNWARD-ONLY, hand\r\n1171:# nonzero, not even under --strict (D-7 stays in force; only the measurement\r\n1260:# architecture readers — check_trajectory, the dashboard, check_doc_refs;\r\n1349:smoke = python project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py --strict && python project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py --strict && python project-trajectory/scripts/check_docs.py --root . --stale && python scripts/check_resync.py && python scripts/check_smoke_budget.py --mode warn\r\n1350:smoke.desc = the per-commit bar: both strict lints, the docs staleness check and the RESYNC entry check, then the smoke tier, run once; a red tier fails the bar, and its CPU seconds and wall time are reported against [smoke-budget] but judged only by the audit; a lint red stops the bar before the tier runs\r\n1353:trajectory = python project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py --strict\r\n1354:trajectory.desc = the trajectory SSOT check, strict\r\n\r\n\r\ndef symbol_findings(root):\r\n    \"\"\"`(dangling, untraced, advisory, unbound)` for the LLR `CodeSymbol` ANCHOR rule\r\n    (WI-429, approved as built by owner ruling OI-20 2026-08-13).\r\n\r\n    THE RULE: a live LLR row must carry at least ONE identifier-shaped\r\n    `CodeSymbol` token that BINDS in one of the `.py` modules its `Module` cell\r\n    names. Everything else about the cell is filed untraced.\r\n\r\n    THE GRAMMAR — WHAT A `CodeSymbol` CELL MAY NAME (ruled with the\r\n    approval, OI-20, on the conservative default; before it the cell had no\r\n    stated grammar at all, which is the rot the census measured). ADMISSIBLE:\r\n\r\n    - a RESOLVABLE CODE SYMBOL — a function, class, method or constant that\r\n      exists at module scope in a named module (private `_`-names and constants\r\n      included: the oracle is `gen_arch_map.module_bindings`, not the rendered\r\n      public-API map);\r\n    - a MODULE PATH — the module itself is the realization artifact;\r\n    - a designed PART SOURCE — a physical part authored as parametric code.\r\n      It BINDS like any other symbol, so admitting it costs this rule nothing.\r\n      (A BOUGHT part never reaches this tier at all: it lives as a `PART` row\r\n      owned by an `IF` row.)\r\n\r\n    NOT ADMISSIBLE: a GENERATED ARTIFACT (its generator is the realization, and\r\n    the artifact is downstream of it) or a PROSE CONTRACT (a description of\r\n    behaviour, however accurate, is not a name). This is why four live rows stay\r\n    HONESTLY UNFOUNDED rather than being founded by widening the grammar to fit\r\n    them — a cell naming a local variable, a never-existing label or an HTML\r\n    attribute is out of scope for the cell, not a defect in the check.\r\n\r\n    THE WEIGHT OF THIS RULE — IT IS THE DECOMPOSITION FLOOR. Under OI-21's\r\n    ladder, rung 4 (LLR definition) TERMINATES WHERE A TOKEN BINDS. A\r\n    requirement that still needs allocating to sub-parts is an SR for that\r\n    sub-scope; one that binds to code is the bottom. So this is not only a\r\n    check on a cell — it is the computation that decides when decomposition is\r\n    FINISHED, which is why it is hard under `--strict` (an advisory version\r\n    would make D-9's `Founded` rung vacuous for one of four tiers).\r\n\r\n    WHY AN ANCHOR AND NOT PER-TOKEN. This is the discharge test D-9 leaves open\r\n    for the LLR tier — `Status = Founded` means \"the artifacts this row calls\r\n    for exist\", and for an LLR (which has no children in this schema) that is\r\n    resolving code. A per-token rule answers a different, harsher question —\r\n    \"is every name in this cell exact\" — and on real data it reds 31 of 149\r\n    rows, 18 of them for tokens that were never symbol claims: a local, an\r\n    attribute, a CSS variable, a prose fragment. That is a check that reds the\r\n    tree on arrival, enforcing a grammar no ruling ever gave the cell. The\r\n    anchor is the same trade ruling R2 made on the sibling cell: validate the\r\n    coarse claim (the code is real, at a module that is real, under a name that\r\n    is really there), rule the fine claim prose — and COUNT the fine misses so\r\n    a tightening stays possible instead of being hidden.\r\n\r\n    The `;` list in `Module` is read as a UNION, never as a positional pairing\r\n    with the symbol list. One live row (LLR-080) does pair them positionally,\r\n    nothing states that convention, and a union can only ever be the safer\r\n    reading: it accepts both authorings and invents no failure. The union\r\n    decides the anchor verdict only; a listed module holding none of the row's\r\n    code is counted apart, untraced-class (`unbound_modules`).\r\n\r\n    Skips are deliberate and each costs a stated thing: a placeholder row\r\n    (`*-000`) so a scaffold stays copy-ready; a non-`.py` module (a hook, a\r\n    shell template) because there is no Python name to bind; a module absent\r\n    from disk because the PATH tier already owns that finding and reporting it\r\n    twice would double-count one rot; an unparseable module because a syntax\r\n    error is `--strict-parse`'s finding, not this one.\r\n\r\n    THE NON-`.py` SKIP IS REPORTED, NOT SILENT (OI-20's ruling, the OI-28\r\n    guard). A row whose `Module` names ONLY non-Python modules used to discharge\r\n    by DEFAULT with no output at all — a fail-open, and one that matters more\r\n    now that template paths are ruled admissible in `Module` as realization\r\n    artifacts. Such a row now returns an ADVISORY naming the row and its\r\n    modules: skipped-with-reason, discharge NOT computed. Warn-only, never the\r\n    exit code, not even under `--strict` — the skip itself is unchanged and\r\n    still correct (there is no Python name to bind); only its VISIBILITY is.\r\n    The other two skips keep their silence because each is another tier's\r\n    finding, already reported there.\"\"\"\r\n    rel = LLR_REL\r\n    bad, untraced, advisory = [], [], []\r\n    if spine_carrier.resolve(root / rel) is None:\r\n        return bad, untraced, advisory, []\r\n    cache, anchored = {}, []\r\n    for row in spine_carrier.load(root / rel, \"LLR-ID\"):\r\n        rid = (row.get(\"LLR-ID\") or \"\").strip()\r\n        if not rid or rid.endswith(\"-000\"):\r\n            continue\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
